The same “text + metadata filter” pattern extends naturally to location: “restaurants within 2km” or “sites inside this delivery zone” are just another filter clause, but the underlying structure isn’t an inverted index at all.
Two field types cover essentially every geo use case:








Field type
Stores
Typical use




geo_point
A single {lat, lon}
A store, a user, a delivery address


geo_shape
Arbitrary GeoJSON - polygon, multipolygon, linestring
A delivery zone, a country border, a service area



The common pairing the field type table implies - and the one worth calling out explicitly - is indexed polygon, query point: a geo_shape polygon is indexed once per delivery zone or site boundary, and each incoming query supplies a geo_point (a user’s lat/long) to test containment against it:
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "filter": [
        { "geo_shape": {
            "delivery_zone": {
              "shape": { "type": "point", "coordinates": [-0.611, 49.187] },
              "relation": "contains"
            }
        }}
      ]
    }
  }
}
relation controls the topological test: intersects (any overlap), within (the query shape is fully inside the indexed shape), contains (the indexed shape fully contains the query shape - what “is this point inside this zone” needs), or disjoint. The same query direction reverses just as easily - index geo_point locations (stores, addresses) and query with a geo_shape polygon (a hand-drawn map selection) using intersects.
For the much more common “nearest N” case, geo_point plus a geo_distance filter is lighter weight than shapes entirely:
{ "geo_distance": { "distance": "5km", "location": { "lat": 49.18, "lon": -0.37 } } }
with geo_bounding_box as the cheaper variant when the front end just needs “everything currently in the map viewport” rather than a true radius.
How it’s actually indexed. Since 6.6, both geo_point and geo_shape are indexed using Lucene’s BKD tree (Block K-D tree) rather than the older geohash-prefix approach - the same multi-dimensional binary-tree structure Lucene uses for numeric range queries, recursively partitioning space into bounding boxes so a bounding-box or distance query only has to descend the branches that could possibly overlap, instead of scanning every point. This is why geo filters stay cheap even over hundreds of millions of points: cost scales with the size of the query region, not the size of the index.
Tunables worth knowing:

distance_type (arc vs plane) - arc is geodesically accurate (accounts for Earth’s curvature) and the default; plane is faster and fine at small distances (city-scale) where the error is negligible.
precision on a geohash_grid aggregation - controls the geohash cell size when bucketing points into a map cluster/heatmap view; higher precision means smaller cells and more buckets.
ignore_malformed - lets ingestion continue past bad coordinates instead of rejecting the whole document, worth enabling for any pipeline consuming external/scraped location data.

Most Python backends end up choosing between three communication styles: REST, GraphQL, and WebSocket. They solve different problems, and FastAPI happens to support all three cleanly, which makes it a good way to compare them side by side.


The default choice for CRUD-style resources. Simple, cacheable, and easy to reason about.
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/users/{user_id}")
def get_user(user_id: int):
    return {"id": user_id, "name": "Alexandre"}



A single endpoint where the client specifies exactly what fields it needs, avoiding over- or under-fetching. Here with Strawberry:
import strawberry
from strawberry.fastapi import GraphQLRouter

@strawberry.type
class User:
    id: int
    name: str

@strawberry.type
class Query:
    @strawberry.field
    def user(self, user_id: int) -&gt; User:
        return User(id=user_id, name="Alexandre")

schema = strawberry.Schema(query=Query)
app.include_router(GraphQLRouter(schema), prefix="/graphql")



A persistent, bidirectional connection - the right tool when the server needs to push data without the client asking first.
from fastapi import WebSocket

@app.websocket("/ws")
async def websocket_endpoint(websocket: WebSocket):
    await websocket.accept()
    while True:
        data = await websocket.receive_text()
        await websocket.send_text(f"Echo: {data}")




REST - public APIs, CRUD-heavy resources, anything that benefits from HTTP caching.
GraphQL - nested or variable data shapes, multiple client types (web/mobile) with different needs.
WebSocket - real-time features like chat, live dashboards, or notifications.

They’re not mutually exclusive - a single FastAPI app can serve a REST API, a GraphQL endpoint, and a WebSocket route at once, and it’s common to mix them depending on the feature.

